  "name": "Question 8",
  "problem": "The vertex of the parabola $y=a{x}^{2}+bx+c$ is the point $(-2,-2).$ Given that $D$ is the parabola's discriminant and $c>0,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $D>0$\n\nII. $D<0$\n\nIII. $D=0$",
  "worked_solution": "We know the location of the vertex and that the $y$-intercept lies above the $x$-axis. So, let's sketch our parabola based on this information.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14266874/assets/87892024228b06460ff187e938abb85ebb1e4a6258260ec2eabee907f7a47e31.png)\n\nSince the vertex $(-2,-2)$ is below the $x$-axis and not lying on the $x$-axis, the curve has either no roots or two distinct roots. Therefore, we must have $D\\ne 0.$\n\nOn the other hand, since $c>0,$ the $y$-intercept is above the $x$-axis. So, the curve must intersect the $x$-axis in at least one place as it traverses from the vertex below the $x$-axis to the $y$-intercept above the $x$-axis. Hence, $D≮0.$\n\nTherefore, $D>0$ and only statement I is true.",
